    fullStory: [
      `David Dinkins governed New York at a moment when the city seemed to be arguing with itself in public. The late 1980s and early 1990s brought fiscal pressure, high crime, AIDS deaths, homelessness, racial conflict, housing strain, and bitter debates over policing. Dinkins became mayor in that storm, not after it had passed.`,
      `He was born in Trenton, New Jersey, in 1927. He served as a Montford Point Marine during World War II, part of the generation of Black Marines who trained under segregation and later received Congressional Gold Medal recognition. After the war, he graduated from Howard University and Brooklyn Law School. His path into politics moved through Democratic organizations, law practice, the New York State Assembly, the Board of Elections, City Clerk, and Manhattan borough president. By the time he ran for mayor, he was not a sudden outsider. He was a patient institutional politician.`,
      `In 1989, Dinkins defeated incumbent mayor Edward Koch in the Democratic primary and then defeated Rudolph Giuliani in the general election. His victory made him the first Black mayor of New York City, a city with global reach and brutal local politics. The symbolism was enormous. So were the expectations.`,
      `Dinkins inherited a city under fiscal strain. The NYC Department of Records notes a combined deficit of $6.8 billion in fiscal years 1991 and 1992. He used budget cuts and tax increases, persuaded businesses to stay, and tried to keep public services alive in a downturn. These were not glamorous choices. They were governing choices, and they made almost everyone unhappy in different ways.`,
      `Public safety dominated his term. Homicides peaked around 1990. Dinkins backed the Safe Streets, Safe City plan, which expanded police staffing and criminal-justice resources while also funding youth and community programs. Crime fell during and after his administration, though later political narratives often denied him credit. The story is complicated: more policing raised questions in Black and Latino communities, but residents also demanded safety. Dinkins had to govern inside that tension.`,
      `Race relations tested him constantly. The boycott of a Korean-owned grocery in Flatbush, the Crown Heights crisis in 1991, and the national reaction to the Rodney King beating and verdict all shaped the public view of his mayoralty. Critics accused him of weakness. Supporters argued that he was trying to avoid turning civic conflict into spectacle. He often chose restraint, and restraint can look different depending on whether someone wants calm, punishment, or political theater.`,
      `His administration also built things. Dinkins kept libraries open longer, restored school-construction funding, supported Beacon Schools that used school buildings for after-hours community programs, expanded affordable housing commitments, and negotiated an agreement keeping the U.S. Open in Queens for decades. He signed strong anti-apartheid legislation after Nelson Mandela chose New York as his first U.S. stop following his release from prison.`,
      `Dinkins lost the 1993 rematch to Giuliani. The loss hardened a public story that treated him as a transitional figure before a tougher era. That story leaves too much out. It minimizes policy work, fiscal management, housing, schools, anti-apartheid politics, and the fact that many problems associated with his administration had roots well before he entered City Hall.`,
      `After leaving office, Dinkins taught at Columbia University's School of International and Public Affairs and remained a public elder in city politics. He died in 2020. His mayoralty still asks a live question: how does a city remember a Black leader who governed complexity rather than selling simple certainty?`,
      `Dinkins did not solve New York. No mayor does. He gave the city a language of mosaic at a time when it was easier to campaign on fracture. That choice was not naive. It was a governing philosophy under pressure.`
    ],
